Yeah. The body uses pressure differences to move blood, and electrochemical gradients to move ions. Both are ways of storing the potential to make something happen, then controlling where and when it happens.

System	What drives the flow	What flows	What controls it
Electrical circuit	Voltage difference	Electric charge	Resistance, switches, regulators
Circulation	Pressure difference	Blood	The heart, valves, and changes in vessel diameter
Cell membrane	Electrical and concentration differences	Ions such as sodium, potassium, and calcium	Ion channels and transporters

The last row is where the analogy becomes literal. Your cells really do have a voltage across their membranes. In a neuron at rest, sodium is more concentrated outside, and the inside is electrically negative relative to the outside. Both differences favor sodium moving inward. Open the right channels and sodium rushes in, changing the voltage. That change opens channels farther down the membrane, so the signal travels. The sodium entering at one spot only has to cross the membrane there. Each stretch of the neuron continues the signal using its own local gradient.

That’s the part I’d steal for Pantheon. A signal can travel by releasing energy that’s already available at each stop. You don’t have to push the whole reaction from the origin. One person asks a question that gives a room permission to say what everyone has been noticing. One line in a story gives a reader enough to reconstruct a scene out of their own memories. In each case, the source supplies a trigger. The receiver supplies much of what comes next.

Your fishing analogy adds the other half. The sentence is the cast, but the pull depends on a difference that was already there. A need to be understood. A desire to prove yourself. A question you’ve been waiting for somebody to ask. The same sentence can pass right by one person and set off a whole chain in another.

And this gives unequipped a more precise meaning. You can have the source and the signal and still be missing the channel. You can also have the channel wide open and be missing the resources that would flow through it. Those are different problems. Before you add pressure, ask what’s already available, what would let it move, and where it needs to go.